[
  {
    "knowledge_point": {
      "db_id": 17592186068697,
      "id": "3f92e521-e7ab-56ba-8eac-0484b140b1a2",
      "title": "Determining the Behavior of a Function in the Neighborhood of a Point"
    },
    "question": {
      ":question/id": "09b23464-fdf0-52ae-9e2f-0decd5c5ccc6",
      ":question/problem": "![](images/d9/d9db7f2a491ae3065dbe8434a71d378c4fed8fec16c82e2fd76dcb209c786809.png)\n\nThe graph of $y=f(x)$ is shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $f(x)$ is defined at $x=1$\n\n- **II.** ${lim}_{x\\to \\,1}f(x)$ exists\n\n- **III.** $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=1$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "By inspecting the graph, we can conclude the following:\n\n- Statement I is false. From the graph we can see that no value has been assigned to $f(x)$ at $x=1.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\,{1}^{-}}f(x) & =1, \\\\ {lim}_{x\\to \\,{1}^{+}}f(x) & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, \n${lim}_{x\\to \\,1}f(x)=1.$\n\n- Statement III is false. The function is not even defined at $x=1.$ The graph contains a break at $x=1.$ It's impossible to draw the graph of $f(x)$ without taking our pencil off of the paper.",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
      ":question/answer-fields": [
        {
          ":answer-field/id": "4be2ad13-2ecf-579f-a061-23d761800bf3",
          ":answer-field/key": "I",
