    },
    "question": {
      ":question/id": "a944a074-a74b-5abf-910f-bb6e0729aef6",
      ":question/problem": "![](images/c1/c132c73b7ca4759882959f9df42110c36e31f3c8af27afa02c0caa20f5f94d52.png)\n\nWhich of the following statements are true with respect to the function $y=f(x)$ whose graph is plotted above.\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $f(x)$ is defined at $x=1$\n- **II.** ${lim}_{x\\to \\,1}f(x)=f(1)$\n- **III.** $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=1$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "By inspecting the graph, we can conclude the following:\n\n- Statement I is true. We have $f(1)=1.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, from the graph we see that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\,{1}^{-}}f(x) & =1, \\\\ {lim}_{x\\to \\,{1}^{+}}f(x) & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, ${lim}_{x\\to \\,1}f(x)=1=f(1).$\n\n- Statement III is true. The graph of $f(x)$ can be drawn without taking our pencil off of the paper in the neighborhood of $x=1.$",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
      ":question/answer-fields": [
