  {
    "knowledge_point": {
      "db_id": 17592186068697,
      "id": "3f92e521-e7ab-56ba-8eac-0484b140b1a2",
      "title": "Determining the Behavior of a Function in the Neighborhood of a Point"
    },
    "question": {
      ":question/id": "4fa41472-eecc-57a0-9592-19490ea449ce",
      ":question/problem": "![](images/51/5143df65d0f99c6511dc8ab13262831669c88535e19c986dacdb364e04847f58.png)\n\nWhich of the following statements are true with respect to the function $y=f(x)$ whose graph is plotted above.\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $f(x)$ is defined at $x=2$\n- **II.** ${lim}_{x\\to \\,2}f(x)\\ne f(2)$\n- **III.** $f(x)$ has a discontinuity at $x=2$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "By inspecting the graph, we can conclude the following:\n\n- Statement I is true. We have $f(2)=2.$\n\n- Statement II is false. Indeed, from the graph we see that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\,{2}^{-}}f(x) & =2, \\\\ {lim}_{x\\to \\,{2}^{+}}f(x) & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, ${lim}_{x\\to \\,2}f(x)=2=f(2).$\n\n- Statement III is false. The graph of the function doesn't have a break at $x=2$ and we can draw the graph of $f(x)$ around $x=2$ without taking our pencil off of the paper.",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
      ":question/answer-fields": [
        {
          ":answer-field/id": "6839baf0-df88-500e-91ec-8751a4a887f0",
          ":answer-field/key": "I",
          ":answer-field/type": ":answer-field.type/checkbox",
          ":answer-field/correct": [
