  {
    "knowledge_point": {
      "db_id": 17592186068697,
      "id": "3f92e521-e7ab-56ba-8eac-0484b140b1a2",
      "title": "Determining the Behavior of a Function in the Neighborhood of a Point"
    },
    "question": {
      ":question/id": "2174ff30-e90f-5b19-b792-ab065a7ab5c6",
      ":question/problem": "![](images/bd/bdcfa35fb04028cbff32992b41214c878443d3815b676a0cf046f5a99bd771bd.png)\n\nThe graph of $y=f(x)$ is shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $f(x)$ is **not** defined at $x=-2$\n- **II.** ${lim}_{x\\to \\,-2}f(x)=f(-2)$\n- **III.** $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=-2$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "By inspecting the graph, we can conclude the following:\n\n- Statement I is false. Indeed, $f(x)$ is defined at $x=-2$ and $f(-2)=5.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, from the graph we see that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\,-{2}^{-}}f(x) & =5, \\\\ {lim}_{x\\to \\,-{2}^{+}}f(x) & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, ${lim}_{x\\to \\,-2}f(x)=5=f(-2).$\n\n- Statement III is true. The graph of $f(x)$ can be drawn without taking our pencil off of the paper in the neighborhood of $x=-2.$",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
      ":question/answer-fields": [
        {
          ":answer-field/id": "a09cb984-c7d1-5cbb-bebe-f47054526145",
